    "q-161765": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161765",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
