    "q-161771": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161771",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14178392/assets/2fd023af7c229d813344ee366b384a5917b69df0d06fabff6466b9ebcc199bb9.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0,$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14178392/assets/090f210768ffd4a6be529860b54a2906e265427b7d85bcbb07e3101c801740ba.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=\\pi /2}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =\\pi }^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{7}{\\rho }^{7}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}(128-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{128}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
